1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:05,396
That 80 Plus Gold sticker on your power supply measures exactly one thing.

2
00:00:05,676 --> 00:00:07,045
And it isn't quality.

3
00:00:07,795 --> 00:00:09,260
It measures efficiency.

4
00:00:09,540 --> 00:00:12,809
That's how much wall power actually reaches your parts.

5
00:00:13,088 --> 00:00:14,524
The rest turns into heat.

6
00:00:15,274 --> 00:00:20,312
Testers load the unit at 20, 50 and 100 percent of its rating.

7
00:00:20,592 --> 00:00:23,883
Gold needs 90 percent efficiency at half load.

8
00:00:24,163 --> 00:00:25,761
Bronze needs 85.

9
00:00:26,511 --> 00:00:28,254
Here's what the badge leaves out.

10
00:00:28,534 --> 00:00:36,409
How steady the voltages stay, how much ripple noise gets through, and the protections that save your parts when something fails.

11
00:00:36,689 --> 00:00:39,505
Makers can even send in hand-picked samples.

12
00:00:40,255 --> 00:00:43,520
So a badly built unit can still wear a Gold badge.

13
00:00:43,800 --> 00:00:48,546
Before you buy, look for an independent review that actually load tests it.

14
00:00:49,296 --> 00:00:53,025
The full story is in our video, Power Supplies Explained.

